Issues.Body:
## Summary

- generalize browser and HTTP startup values behind host-neutral provider/consumer interfaces
- run ordered host initializers across static SSR, interactive Server circuits, and WebAssembly
- migrate navigation initialization and Server navigation-service JS-runtime attachment to the pipeline

## API status

The public API in this PR is approved in #68957. #68956 only clarifies API-review process wording and is not the source of API approval.

```diff
namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Hosting;

+public interface IHostInitializer
+{
+    int Order { get; }
+    bool RequiresJSInterop { get; }
+    Task InitializeAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken = default);
+}
+
+public interface IHostStartupValues
+{
+    string? GetValue(string key);
+    string GetRequired(string key);
+}
+
+public interface IBrowserStartupValueProvider
+{
+    IReadOnlyList<string> Keys { get; }
+}
+
+public interface IHttpContextStartupValueProvider
+{
+    IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> GetValues(HttpContext httpContext);
+}
```

`IHostInitializer.Order` defaults to `0`; `RequiresJSInterop` defaults to `false`.

## Behavior and compatibility

- lower initializer orders run first; ties preserve DI registration order
- failures and cancellation stop initialization and flow through existing host error paths
- Server defers the ordered suffix beginning with the first JS-required initializer, completes it at most once through a nonblocking client/server handshake, and gates both initial descriptors and root-component updates
- legacy Server clients retain the existing `StartCircuit`/`ResumeCircuit` paths and framework navigation attachments
- new clients renegotiate startup-value support for start and resume, preserving mixed-version and cross-server resume behavior
- browser keys are validated dot-separated property paths and are resolved without `eval`; duplicate, dangerous, missing, or non-string values are rejected
- HTTP startup values remain request-local and are never emitted to the browser
- host selection uses implementation-private DI keys; no public host-name convention is introduced

## Scope

Intentionally excludes ESM bundle architecture, Rollup configuration, render-mode plugins, `JSModuleRegistry`, component migrations, script-mode E2E infrastructure, pipeline YAML, `SsrBrowserOptions.EnableEnhancedNavigation`, and unrelated prototype changes.

## Validation

- `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Tests`: 1 focused test passed
- `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Endpoints.Tests`: 12 focused tests passed
- `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Server.Tests`: 110 focused tests passed
- `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.Tests`: 36 focused tests passed
- Web.JS Jest: 26 focused tests passed
- Web.JS debug and production bundles built successfully
- Components E2E: `HostInitializersRunBeforeInteractiveServerRendering` passed
- interactive sample validation covered static SSR, Interactive Server, Interactive WebAssembly, and Interactive Auto; Server/WebAssembly/Auto observed browser values, ordered JS readiness, and a real state-changing interaction

The repository-wide `eng\build.cmd` reached installer packaging but could not complete because `wix.exe` is unavailable in the local environment; affected Components projects and focused tests build successfully.

## Background and Motivation

<!--
We welcome API proposals! We have a process to evaluate the value and shape of new API. There is an overview of our process [here](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/main/docs/APIReviewProcess.md). This template will help us gather the information we need to start the review process.
First, please describe the purpose and value of the new API here.
-->

Blazor currently initializes host services through host-specific, hardcoded sequences in static SSR/prerendering, interactive Server circuits, and WebAssembly. Adding or reordering an initialization step requires coordinated changes in each host, and component libraries have no common extension point for initialization that must happen after host inputs are available but before components render.

This proposal introduces two related primitives:

1. Startup-value providers collect string values from the current `HttpContext` or browser. Hosts expose the resulting values through a common service, including values transported as part of interactive startup.
2. Ordered host initializers consume those values and initialize services consistently in endpoint rendering, Server circuits, and `WebAssemblyHost`.

The initial framework consumers are the existing navigation manager, navigation interception, JS-runtime attachment, and scroll-to-location-hash initialization steps. The goal is to generalize their existing behavior rather than add a new application bootstrap model.

This follows the scenario described in #30304. #68956 discusses process wording only and does not constitute API approval. The browser-options API approved in #66393 is related server-to-browser configuration, but it does not provide these startup-value or initialization extension points.

## Proposed API

<!--
Please provide the specific public API signature diff that you are proposing. For example:
```diff
namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http;

public static class HttpResponseWritingExtensions
{
+    public Task WriteAsync(this HttpResponse response, StringBuilder builder);
}
```
You may find the [Framework Design Guidelines](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/master/docs/coding-guidelines/framework-design-guidelines-digest.md) helpful.
-->

```diff
+using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http;
+
+namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Hosting;
+
+public interface IHostInitializer
+{
+    int Order { get; }
+    bool RequiresJSInterop { get; }
+    Task InitializeAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken = default);
+}
+
+public interface IHostStartupValues
+{
+    string? GetValue(string key);
+    string GetRequired(string key);
+}
+
+public interface IBrowserStartupValueProvider
+{
+    IReadOnlyList<string> Keys { get; }
+}
+
+public interface IHttpContextStartupValueProvider
+{
+    IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> GetValues(HttpContext httpContext);
+}
```

`IHostInitializer` and `IHostStartupValues` would be defined in `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components`. `IBrowserStartupValueProvider` would be defined in `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web`, and `IHttpContextStartupValueProvider` in `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Endpoints`; all use the `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Hosting` namespace.

The intended defaults are `Order == 0` and `RequiresJSInterop == false`, implemented as default interface members so simple initializers only implement `InitializeAsync`.

The following behavioral contracts are part of the proposal and need API-review agreement:

- Lower `Order` values execute first. Initializers with equal order preserve DI registration order.
- An exception or cancellation aborts host initialization and is surfaced through the host's existing startup/error path.
- `RequiresJSInterop` defers an initializer until JS interop is available. This is most significant for Server circuits, where interop is unavailable during the initial hub startup operation.
- Browser provider keys are dot-separated JavaScript property paths resolved from `window`, such as `document.baseURI` and `location.href`.
- Browser and HTTP values are represented as ordinal string keys and string values. Duplicate-key behavior must be deterministic; the proposed implementation rejects conflicting providers rather than silently overwriting values.
- Browser startup-value transport must remain backward compatible with clients and servers that do not send or understand the generalized values.
- Shared initializers are registered as normal DI services. The prototype used keyed DI registrations for host-specific initializers; API review should decide whether that convention needs a typed public host identifier or public constants rather than magic strings such as `"Server"` and `"Static"`.

This proposal intentionally does not add `SsrBrowserOptions.EnableEnhancedNavigation` or any ESM/module-registry APIs. Any additional public option required by implementation would need its own approved surface.

## Usage Examples

<!--
Please provide code examples that highlight how the proposed API additions are meant to be consumed.
This will help suggest whether the API has the right shape to be functional, performant and useable.
You can use code blocks like this:
```csharp
// some lines of code here
```
-->

A library can declare browser values and initialize a service after the active host has populated them:

```csharp
services.AddSingleton<IBrowserStartupValueProvider, BrowserLocationStartupValues>();
services.AddSingleton<IHostInitializer, BrowserLocationInitializer>();

internal sealed class BrowserLocationStartupValues : IBrowserStartupValueProvider
{
    public IReadOnlyList<string> Keys { get; } =
        ["document.baseURI", "location.href"];
}

internal sealed class BrowserLocationInitializer(
    IHostStartupValues startupValues,
    NavigationManager navigationManager) : IHostInitializer
{
    public Task InitializeAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        navigationManager.Initialize(
            startupValues.GetRequired("document.baseURI"),
            startupValues.GetRequired("location.href"));

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

An initializer that invokes JavaScript declares that requirement so Server circuits can defer it until interop is available:

```csharp
internal sealed class NavigationInterceptionInitializer(
    INavigationInterception navigationInterception) : IHostInitializer
{
    public int Order => 100;
    public bool RequiresJSInterop => true;

    public Task InitializeAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken = default) =>
        navigationInterception.EnableNavigationInterceptionAsync();
}
```

An HTTP provider can expose request-specific values during static SSR and prerendering:

```csharp
services.AddSingleton<IHttpContextStartupValueProvider, RequestPathStartupValues>();

internal sealed class RequestPathStartupValues : IHttpContextStartupValueProvider
{
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> GetValues(HttpContext httpContext) =>
        new Dictionary<string, string>
        {
            ["request.pathBase"] = httpContext.Request.PathBase,
            ["request.path"] = httpContext.Request.Path,
        };
}
```

## Alternative Designs

<!--
Were there other options you considered, such as alternative API shapes?
How does this compare to analogous APIs in other ecosystems and libraries?
-->

- Keep host-specific hardcoded initialization. This avoids new public API but continues requiring every feature to modify endpoint rendering, Server, WebAssembly, and potentially WebView separately.
- Define separate initializers for `HttpContext`, Circuit, and `WebAssemblyHost`, as originally outlined in #30304. This gives each initializer more host context but forces libraries to implement several host-specific contracts and couples abstractions to concrete hosts.
- Add callbacks to each host builder. This follows existing host APIs but does not provide one portable library extension point and does not naturally cover static SSR endpoint rendering.
- Keep the pipeline internal. This is sufficient for migrating framework-owned steps but does not solve the library-author scenario described in #30304.
- Use an untyped dictionary service without provider interfaces. This simplifies consumption but leaves each host without a discoverable, composable way to determine which HTTP/browser values must be collected before rendering.

## Risks

<!--
Please mention any risks that to your knowledge the API proposal might entail, such as breaking changes, performance regressions, etc.
-->

- Ordering can create hidden coupling between initializers. Registration-order tie breaking, duplicate registrations, and order ranges need documentation.
- A generic JavaScript-property-path contract exposes browser evaluation semantics as public behavior. Invalid paths and unavailable values need clear diagnostics, and arbitrary expression evaluation must not be allowed.
- Adding startup values to the circuit/bootstrap protocol can break mixed-version clients and servers unless the payload is optional and old fields retain their meaning.
- `RequiresJSInterop` adds a two-phase startup model on Server. The pipeline must guarantee each initializer runs at most once and that components do not observe partially initialized services.
- Startup-value key collisions can make behavior registration-order dependent unless conflicts are rejected.
- Cancellation and initialization failures must flow through each host's existing error boundary without being swallowed or converted into successful startup.
- The keyed-registration approach from the prototype would create a public convention around host-name strings unless the API provides a typed alternative.

When Output Caching or Response Caching varies by a configured query key or header, its cache key does not distinguish between the key/header being absent and the key/header being present with an empty value (such as `?mode=`).

The application layer does distinguish these states through `Request.Query.ContainsKey("mode")` and `Request.Headers.ContainsKey("X-Mode")`.

Example:
```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddOutputCache();
builder.Services.AddResponseCaching();
var app = builder.Build();
app.UseResponseCaching();
app.UseOutputCache();
var counter = 0;
app.MapGet("/output/query", (HttpContext context) =>
{
    return new
    {
        Present = context.Request.Query.ContainsKey("mode"),
        Value = context.Request.Query["mode"].ToString(),
        Counter = Interlocked.Increment(ref counter),
        GeneratedAt = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow
    };
})
.CacheOutput(policy => policy
    .Expire(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(20))
    .SetVaryByQuery("mode"));
app.Run();
```

```text
curl "http://localhost:5000/output/query"
curl "http://localhost:5000/output/query?mode="
```

